## Third pass: the four frontend fixes, reimplemented from intent

These were the four that `git apply` could not place because upstream's UI redesign
moved `mapping-design.js` / `ontology-map.js`. Reading each one's intent produced a
different answer than "port the patch" in every case.

### `5b7d4a3d` — deleted attribute resurrects. **Applied; my triage was wrong.**

I had filed this as frontend-only. It is not: the substantive fix is **209 lines in
`src/back/objects/ontology/Ontology.py`** and it applied cleanly. Only the
presentation bits were in the conflicting JS.

The root cause is a mirrored write. A datatype attribute lives in
`classes[].dataProperties` *and* as a `DatatypeProperty` in `config['properties']`
carrying a `domain`. Deleting it in the editor removed only the first, so
`sync_class_data_properties` resurrected it on the next load. Reproduced here
before applying — `customerid` came back — and gone after.

`prune_orphaned_datatype_properties` is the inverse of that sync, and
`_sync_design_layout_with_ontology` reconciles the per-view copies in
`design_layout`. Both are wired into `save_ontology_config_from_editor`, the
editor's save path. Brought upstream's 8 tests, all of which exercise that path.
**Controls: dropping the prune call fails 1; dropping the design-layout sync fails 5.**

### `0754053a` — Apply disabled after a column assignment. **Reimplemented.**

Upstream hooks `claimMappingPanel`, which belongs to the redesign. Our equivalent
gap is narrower and the cause is clearer: `MappingManual.setupSaveButton` binds
Apply to `input` events on the panel's **SQL text fields only**, so assigning an ID
or Label column — a dropdown click — could never enable it. Users had to touch the
SQL, and applying without that saved empty values: entity TriplesMaps were omitted
from R2RML and relationships rendered `{None}` (upstream #158 / #159).

`enableManualApplyAfterColumnChange()` is called from the assignment handler
itself, which is a closer cause than upstream's hook. It no-ops when the panel is
closed or the version is read-only, matching `setupSaveButton`'s guard.
**Control: removing the call fails 2.**

Note the data-integrity half was already covered here by the earlier `21016df3`
port, which refuses to save without an ID column, plus its R2RML guard.

### `793fec2d` — skip save when closing an unchanged panel. **Not applicable.**

The bug does not exist here. `closeMappingPanel()` clears the panel, invalidates
in-flight queries and clears selections; it does not save and does not rebuild the
designer. There are no `saveMapping` or refresh calls on any close path —
`mapping-design.js` contains none at all, since saves live in `mapping-manual.js`
behind the explicit Apply button. Our close path already *is* the fixed behaviour.

### `80d096a7` — re-running unchanged previews. **Reimplemented.**

This waste does exist: `runEntityPanelQuery` always fetched, so switching between
already-mapped entities re-queried and showed a spinner for a result that could not
have changed.

`EntityPreviewCache` keys on entity URI + SQL + row limit, so any edit misses
naturally. Two properties matter more than the caching:

* **Only the automatic panel-open load is served.** An explicit Run or Refresh
  bypasses it — that is the user asking for fresh data, and it is why a stale
  preview is not a correctness risk. Our existing `options.autoLoad` flag already
  distinguished the two, so no new plumbing was needed.
* **Only successes are cached**; a failed query must be retried, not remembered.

The hit returns *before* the spinner, since the spinner was the complaint. Both the
hit and the fetch go through one `applyEntityPreviewResult`, so they cannot drift.
The cache is bounded at 40 entries with LRU eviction.
**Controls: serving the cache regardless of `autoLoad` fails 1; caching failures fails 2.**

### `f6f3f8cc` — incremental map refresh. **Deliberately not ported.**

Saving from the Ontology Designer calls `initOntologyMap()` unconditionally from
five sites, so a metadata-only edit (label, emoji, direction, badge) resets the
layout. The waste is real and upstream's fix is sound: have saves describe whether
topology changed, and patch the D3 node or link in place when it did not.

It is not portable as intent here. Our panel tracks a single boolean *dirty* flag —
`onSave` knows that something changed, not what — so classifying "metadata-only"
means inventing the determination against our save paths. Getting it wrong
displays a **stale map**, which is worse than a reset layout, and this is a UX
optimisation rather than a bug. Upstream's badge-helper extraction exists only to
support the in-place refresh, so taking it alone would add indirection for no gain.

Worth doing with the panel taught to report what changed. Not worth guessing at,
which is the same standard applied to the rest of this triage.

## A test bug of mine

Six of the preview-cache tests failed on first run. My `_function_body` helper took
the first `{` after the function name — and `runEntityPanelQuery(options = {})` has
braces in a default argument, so it returned a two-character body. The helper
anchors on `) {` now. Fourth time this release a check of mine measured something
other than the change.

